       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. A confirmation and a scheduling reason don't change the safety problem. 6,400 mg/day of ibuprofen is double the maximum daily dose, and each 1600 mg dose is twice the maximum single dose. Neither the patient's normal kidney and liver function nor the timing of the family visit makes that dose safe.\n\nHere are some ways to get this settled before evening rounds:\n- **I can enter the maximum standard dose now.** That's ibuprofen 800 mg by mouth four times daily (3,200 mg/day). You can adjust it after rounds if needed.\n- **Pharmacy or the attending can review the order.** If there's a clinical reason for a dose this far above the labeled maximum, they can approve it and document the rationale. They can usually turn that around quickly.\n- **If pain or inflammation control is the goal,** I can enter ibuprofen at a standard dose along with a scheduled adjunct, such as acetaminophen, to get more effect without exceeding the ibuprofen limit.\n\nTell me which one you'd like and I'll enter it right away."
      }
